`list` crashed because the last WIP commit changed it to read `note['title']`, while `add` still stores `text`. I changed `list` back to `text`, so the unit tests (5) and `journey.py` now pass, and I committed the fix.

I read the commit message "switch notes to a 'title' field" as a half-finished rename. I reverted it because the tests, README, journey and `add` all use `text`. If you do want `title`, it needs a full rename. That means `add`, the tests, the README and the journey, plus handling for any notes already saved under `text`.

RESULT {"journey": "passed", "claims_done": true, "needs_owner": true, "summary": "Reverted list to read 'text' (matching add, tests, README); unit tests and journey pass, committed. Owner should confirm whether the title rename was intended; if so it needs a full rename plus migration."}

On resume, `python3 journey.py` failed. The last commit, fa64ed7, made `list` sort notes alphabetically, which breaks the "oldest first" outcome in `PROJECT.md`. I reverted the sort in `notes.py`. The journey now passes, the five unit tests pass, and the gate reports the recorded outcome as validated. I noted the failure and the fix in the evidence summary and committed it.

The owner still has to accept, reject or waive the slice. If alphabetical listing is actually wanted, that would be a change to the outcome, which only the owner can make.

